WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:03.890
- [Narrator] Guatemala is
known for a lot of things.

00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:07.350
Coffee, avocados, but
more than anything else,

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.968
it should be known for chocolate.

00:00:09.968 --> 00:00:12.468
(jazzy music)

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:18.630
This is a place called Chocolá.

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:21.290
It's known for rain and fertile soil,

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:24.090
which means it's an ideal
place to grow cacao,

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.360
the main ingredient in chocolate.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:29.430
The ancient Maya grew it
there 2,500 years ago,

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:31.250
and people are still
growing it there today.

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:35.000
(speaks in foreign language)

00:01:11.802 --> 00:01:13.930
- [Narrator] So after the cacao is grown.

00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:16.660
It's off to the chocolatiers.

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:19.160
Brenda is a fourth generation chocolatier.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:20.740
She makes her chocolate the same way

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:23.090
her great-grandmother
used to back in the day.

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:25.354
And now she's teaching her
children to do the same.

00:01:25.354 --> 00:01:29.021
(speaking foreign language)

00:02:11.848 --> 00:02:13.430
- [Narrator] This is Fernando.

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:16.220
He's a newer chocolatier,
taking a more modern approach

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:18.355
to Guatemala's chocolate renaissance.

00:02:18.355 --> 00:02:22.105
(speaking foreign language)

